## About this package

This package is part of the ${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_MAJOR}.${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_MINOR}.${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_PATCH} release of Emgu LiteRT. It contains:

- `Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Agents.dll`: ready-made tools for chat agents, as [Microsoft.Extensions.AI](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/ai/microsoft-extensions-ai) functions (`AgentTools`):

| Tool | Factory | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `get_current_date_time` | `AgentTools.CurrentDateTime()` | The local date and time, with the time zone |
| `calculate` | `AgentTools.Calculate()` | Evaluates arithmetic exactly (`Calculator`) - small models get arithmetic wrong |
| `convert_units` | `AgentTools.ConvertUnits()` | Converts length, mass, volume, temperature, speed, area, time, data, energy and pressure (`UnitConverter`) |
| `convert_currency` | `AgentTools.ConvertCurrency()` | Converts money with the European Central Bank's daily rates, from [Frankfurter](https://frankfurter.dev) (no key needed) |
| `read_web_page` | `AgentTools.ReadWebPage()` | The title and main text of a web page (`HtmlText`), fetched directly (no key needed) |
| `remember`, `forget` | `AgentTools.Memory(store)` | Save and delete facts about the user in an `IAgentMemoryStore` (e.g. your app's database; `InMemoryAgentMemoryStore` for a quick start); `AgentTools.MemoryInstructionsAsync(store)` gives the agent the saved ones |
| `find_photos` | `AgentTools.FindPhotos(search)` | Finds the user's photos, given the request in their words; your app's search turns it into what, where, when, kind and camera with `PhotoRequest.Parse`, e.g. over an index built with `EmbeddingGemma2TextVision`, with place names from `PlaceLookup` (offline reverse geocoding from a list of towns) |
| `generate_image` | `AgentTools.GenerateImage(generate)` | Draws a picture from a short English description the model writes, with your app's image generator (e.g. `Emgu.LiteRT.Models`' `ZImageTurbo`), which shows it to the user |
| `get_current_location` | `AgentTools.Location(lookup)` | Where the user is, from a lookup your app provides (e.g. MAUI's Geolocation and Geocoding) |
| `get_weather` | `AgentTools.Weather()` | Current weather and a 3-day forecast from [Open-Meteo](https://open-meteo.com) (no key needed) |
| `search_wikipedia` | `AgentTools.Wikipedia()` | Introductions of the best matching English Wikipedia articles |
| `search_web`, `read_web_page` | `AgentTools.TavilySearch(key)`, `AgentTools.TavilyReadPage(key)` | Web search and page text from [Tavily](https://tavily.com) (an API key is needed) |

The tools don't use LiteRT-LM: they work with any `IChatClient` that calls tools, and with [Microsoft Agent Framework](https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/) agents. They were made for the on-device `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MODELS_NUGET_ID}` language models (through `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_EXTENSIONS_AI_NUGET_ID}`), so their results are kept short. The web tools send what they look up (places, search terms, page addresses) to those services.

```csharp
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Agents;
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Extensions.AI;
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Models;
using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;

using (Gemma4E2B model = new Gemma4E2B())
{
    await model.Init();
    using (IChatClient client = new ChatClientBuilder(model.AsIChatClient()).UseFunctionInvocation().Build())
    {
        ChatOptions options = new ChatOptions
        {
            Tools = new List<AITool> { AgentTools.CurrentDateTime(), AgentTools.Calculate(), AgentTools.Weather(), AgentTools.Wikipedia() }
        };
        ChatResponse response = await client.GetResponseAsync("What's the weather in Paris, and what is 17 * 24.3?", options);
        Console.WriteLine(response.Text);
    }
}
```

Sound monitors (`Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Agents.Audio`): `SoundMonitorTools.Create(...)` gives an agent `create_sound_monitor`, `list_sound_monitors` and `control_sound_monitor` ("tell me when the doorbell rings"), backed by a `SoundMonitorRuntime` that your app feeds with each window's 521 YAMNet class scores (`runtime.Advance(time, scores)`); it confirms sounds (`SoundGroups`: doorbell, dog, baby crying, smoke alarm... as groups of YAMNet classes), and runs your alert callback. The microphone and the classifier are yours - `Emgu.LiteRT.Models`' `YamNet` runs the model.

Also in `Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Agents.Audio`: `AlertSoundLibrary` (named sounds an alert can play, built-in or added by the user, matched loosely by name), `AlertAudioCoordinator` (keeps alert sounds and spoken replies from talking over each other), and for voice chats `VoiceActivityDetector` (decides when a spoken message has ended, from microphone levels) and `SpeechSegmenter` (splits a streaming reply into sentences to read aloud, skipping code and tables).

`TaskPlan` is task mode: a prompt that has the model write a short numbered plan, the parser for it, and the prompts that run each step and write the final answer from the steps' results.

`AgentTools.Describe(call)` describes a tool call (e.g. "Searching Wikipedia for “Ada Lovelace”"), to show while it runs.

## Visual agents and event monitors

`Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Agents.Vision` and `.Events` provide a pure managed framework; the host supplies camera/image input,
detector models, and actions. A language model configures rules through `VisualAgentTools.Create(...)`; it is not
invoked for every frame or event. These components can also be used directly without a language model.

```csharp
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Agents.Vision;
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Agents.Events;

var registry = new VisualAgentRegistry();
registry.Register(yourDetector); // IVisualAgent: Descriptor, PrepareAsync, AnalyzeAsync, Dispose
var runtime = new VisualMonitorRuntime(registry, new[] { yourSoundAction }); // IVisualAction
var rule = runtime.CreateObjectMonitor("person"); // available detector selected by supported canonical class
runtime.SetFeedVisible(true);
runtime.SubmitFrame(frame); // transfers ownership of a timestamped BGR8 VisualFrame, including on drops
// Before hiding the live feed, synchronously invalidate work and cancel actions:
runtime.SetFeedVisible(false);
await runtime.DisposeAsync();
```

For explicit action discovery, register `IVisualActionFactory` implementations in `VisualActionRegistry` and pass
that registry to the runtime. A factory advertises `VisualActionDescriptor` (ID, availability, description and JSON
parameter schema) and validates/binds parameters without executing side effects:

```csharp
var actions = new VisualActionRegistry();
actions.Register(yourSoundFactory);
actions.Register(yourSpeechFactory);
var runtime = new VisualMonitorRuntime(registry, actions, actionCoordinator: yourOptionalCoordinator);
var rule = runtime.CreateObjectMonitor("person", new[] {
    new VisualActionRequest("play_sound", VisualActionParameters.Empty),
    new VisualActionRequest("speak_text", VisualActionParameters.Text("Person detected"))
});
```

The six-tool `VisualAgentTools.Create` overload adds `list_visual_actions` and derives the monitor action-array
schema from registered available descriptors. Pair it with `VisualAgentTools.Instructions`. The legacy five-tool,
sound-only overload remains available with `LegacyInstructions`. Hosts may pass source-generated serializer
options for action argument deserialization. The old single-action runtime overloads are retained.

Action sets reject unknown/unavailable IDs and repeated types; each factory must reject unknown parameters and
invalid values. Complete validation precedes rule creation. Normalized parameters are cloned; equivalent sets
are order independent. `Snapshot().Actions` exposes configuration and `LastActionResults` reports each action's
Queued/Running/Succeeded/Failed/Cancelled/Skipped status for the latest event group.

Groups execute FIFO (sixteen waiting groups maximum); sibling actions launch concurrently and fail independently.
An optional `IVisualActionGroupCoordinator` supplies a disposable resource lease around the entire group,
including all sibling cleanup. Hide/pause/remove cancels every sibling. Hosts must honor cancellation and release
native resources before returning. Stale group results cannot overwrite a newer event's results.

Register adapters before runtime use. `IVisualAgent` returns `VisualAnalysis` containing typed observations (kind,
canonical label, confidence, optional normalized box/text/tracking ID) and source/capture metadata. The runtime
serializes preparation and inference; adapters must honor cancellation, and must not retain borrowed frame pixels.
Still images use `AnalyzeImageAsync` and do not advance monitors or trigger actions. The caller disposes still-image
frames after awaiting completion. Input frames need increasing, monotonic `Timestamp` values within a source;
wall-clock `CapturedAt` is for display, not debounce timing. `Dispose` must permit later re-preparation if the host
reuses an adapter.

Use `CreateMonitor(agentId, condition, actionId, phase)` for other `IVisualCondition` predicates and registered actions.
Conditions emit `Started`, `Updated` (changed count) and `Ended`, sharing an episode ID. Lifecycle states are separate:
Preparing, Watching, Paused, Stopped and Faulted. The default presence policy requires two positive observations within
one second, tolerates brief misses, and ends after at least two negatives spanning 1.5 seconds. A gap over two seconds
resets temporal state without inventing an Ended event. Failed analyses are unknown observations, not absence.

The runtime accepts at most 5 FPS, retains only the newest pending frame, and runs actions on a separate bounded
dispatcher. Actions check cancellation immediately before side effects. Each selected transition is dispatched once
in memory, without automatic retries; this is not a durable exactly-once delivery guarantee. Subscribe to `Changed`
and read `Snapshot()` for readiness, faults and last event/action results; marshal UI updates to the UI thread.
Observers should not throw. `EventOccurred` exposes typed events without an LLM round-trip.

Rules are session-only (maximum eight); equivalent creation requests return the existing rule. Hidden feeds pause
enabled rules and invalidate pending results/actions. Reopening starts fresh confirmation. Explicit user pause survives
feed reopen; detector faults require explicit resume. `Clear()` ends a chat's rules, and `DisposeAsync()` drains work
before releasing model adapters. No pixels, rule definitions or event history are written to disk by this framework.
Text recognized in images is untrusted scene data and must never be executed as instructions.

The MAUI demo supplies COCO SSD MobileNet, a local chime, fixed-text system speech and a `take_snapshot` action.
Snapshot alerts show a fresh camera JPEG in the current session (newest twenty in memory), with explicit manual
save. Images are not sent to the model or saved with chats. Snapshot-only groups leave chat read-aloud playing. Its audio coordinator
interrupts chat read-aloud before alert groups and retains only the latest pending reply until alerts finish. Add YOLO, face detection, OCR or plate-recognition
adapters by implementing the same contracts and advertising only supported capabilities/classes; custom predicates
can consume their observations. No native detector or audio library is a dependency of this package.

